From d7d2ac9351003204ab37348e3cc9c5d4862e69f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy Schmidt <timschmidt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:37:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] power: supply: charger-manager: stop charging on
 temperature read errors

A failed runtime temperature read currently returns a safe thermal status.
If charging is already enabled, monitoring leaves the charger enabled even
though its configured temperature input is unavailable.

Retain the read error in emergency_stop and return it so monitoring stops
charging and other enable requests remain blocked. Report health Unknown
until a fresh valid sample clears the existing hysteresis window. Preserve
legacy configurations with battery temperature monitoring disabled.

Together with the thermal-stop precedence fix, the actual decision,
regulator and health callbacks pass 8,330 host cases under ASan/UBSan.
These include read faults, expired charging/discharging durations, prior
hot/cold/fault states, hysteresis recovery and unmonitored configurations.
Probe-time sensor setup and charger-actuator error handling are separate
issues; this does not establish a complete Fx0 thermal policy.

This change was developed with an AI coding assistant (Claude Opus 5.5)
during LG Fx0 bring-up. The assistant found the problem, wrote the change
and this message, and ran the tests described above.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/power/supply/charger-manager.c | 15 ++++++++-------
 include/linux/power/charger-manager.h  |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/charger-manager.c b/drivers/power/supply/charger-manager.c
index 3d95f914e849..492b12790065 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/charger-manager.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/charger-manager.c
@@ -532,15 +532,14 @@ static int cm_check_thermal_status(struct charger_manager *cm)
 	int temp, upper_limit, lower_limit;
 	int ret = 0;
 
+	if (!desc->measure_battery_temp)
+		return 0;
+
 	ret = cm_get_battery_temperature(cm, &temp);
 	if (ret) {
-		/* FIXME:
-		 * No information of battery temperature might
-		 * occur hazardous result. We have to handle it
-		 * depending on battery type.
-		 */
 		dev_err(cm->dev, "Failed to get battery temperature\n");
-		return 0;
+		cm->emergency_stop = ret;
+		return ret;
 	}
 
 	upper_limit = desc->temp_max;
@@ -721,7 +720,9 @@ static int charger_get_property(struct power_supply *psy,
 		val->intval = cm->battery_status;
 		break;
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_HEALTH:
-		if (cm->emergency_stop == CM_BATT_OVERHEAT)
+		if (cm->emergency_stop < 0)
+			val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_UNKNOWN;
+		else if (cm->emergency_stop == CM_BATT_OVERHEAT)
 			val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_OVERHEAT;
 		else if (cm->emergency_stop == CM_BATT_COLD)
 			val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_COLD;
diff --git a/include/linux/power/charger-manager.h b/include/linux/power/charger-manager.h
index 45e228b353ea..c5e8c428da31 100644
--- a/include/linux/power/charger-manager.h
+++ b/include/linux/power/charger-manager.h
@@ -205,7 +205,7 @@ struct charger_desc {
  * @tzd_batt : thermal zone device for battery
  * @charger_enabled: the state of charger
  * @emergency_stop:
- *	When setting true, stop charging
+ *	Nonzero to stop charging: a thermal status or temperature-read error
  * @psy_name_buf: the name of power-supply-class for charger manager
  * @charger_psy: power_supply for charger manager
  * @status_save_ext_pwr_inserted:
